<type>(<scope>): <imperative subject, no trailing period, <= 72 chars>

## User Requests
- "<the ask, verbatim or closely paraphrased, that drove this commit>"

## Changes
- `<path or area>` — <what changed>

## Technical Details
<Why this approach; decisions made and alternatives rejected; anything a
reviewer would otherwise have to reverse-engineer from the diff.>

; ==============================================================================
; DocuShift commit template
; Lines starting with ';' are stripped (core.commentChar=; is set locally). Delete any section you do not need.
; ==============================================================================
;
; SUBJECT
;   <type>(<scope>): <subject>
;
;   type   feat     new capability
;          fix      bug fix
;          docs     documentation only (incl. CONTEXT.md ledger entries)
;          refactor behaviour-preserving restructure
;          test     adding or correcting tests
;          build    pyproject, deps, packaging, .gitignore
;          chore    tooling, config, housekeeping
;          perf     performance work
;          revert   reverts a previous commit
;
;   scope  Pipeline stage or module, matching the source tree:
;          catalog | discovery | downloader | extractor | engines |
;          transforms | aem | sync | cli | state | config | taxonomy |
;          reporting | utils | docs
;          Omit the scope if the change is genuinely cross-cutting.
;
;   Imperative mood ("add", not "added"/"adds"). No trailing period.
;
; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; ## User Requests
;   The prompt(s) that motivated this commit, so intent survives the diff.
;   Quote the ask rather than restating the outcome — the outcome is in
;   ## Changes. Include follow-up corrections; they are often the most
;   informative part of the history. One bullet per distinct request.
;
;   Example:
;     - "the list should be json or csv as i prefer to edit csv when the
;        list is too large"
;     - "keep in mind while we are planning and executing, keep updating
;        the documentation and context as well"
;
; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; ## Changes
;   One bullet per file or coherent area. Say what changed, not how it
;   works — mechanism belongs in ## Technical Details. Group related files
;   under one bullet rather than mirroring the diff file-by-file.
;
;   Example:
;     - `docs/architecture.md` — §3 rewritten from JSON schema to the CSV
;       schema with column-ownership and provenance tables
;     - `config/` — `catalog.json` retired; `products.csv` + `versions.csv`
;       added
;
; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; ## Technical Details
;   The reviewer's shortcut. Cover whichever apply:
;     - design decisions and the alternatives rejected, with the reason
;     - trade-offs knowingly accepted
;     - migration/compatibility impact on existing data or config
;     - known gaps, follow-ups, or anything deliberately left undone
;     - how it was verified (tests added, commands run, or "not yet run")
;
;   State plainly when something is unverified. Do not imply testing that
;   did not happen.
;
; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; BEFORE COMMITTING
;   [ ] CONTEXT.md §3 ledger has a row for any decision made here
;   [ ] CONTEXT.md §4 and docs/planning.md checkboxes match the filesystem
;   [ ] docs/architecture.md reflects any schema or pipeline change
;   [ ] docs/user-guide.md reflects any CLI or config-file change
;
; FOOTER
;   Reference issues with "Refs: #123" / "Closes: #123".
;   For AI-assisted commits, keep the trailer:
;     Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
; ==============================================================================
